import {dictionary} from './translations-en.js';
export function registerTourEnglish(steps,direction='ensemble'){
 const translated=[
 ['Why I started with ensemble playing','I play piano and accompany other musicians. I began exploring remote MIDI ensembles because I wanted people in different places to rehearse together. This website is a later piano-learning prototype, separate from the remote ensemble system. I would like to use it to show you a few questions I am still working through. Please pause and explore whenever you like.'],
 ['Choose a piece in the full-screen library','Please choose a piece you know. A familiar melody makes it easier to hear when something is wrong. We will follow the same piece through the demonstration.\n\nSearch for a title above and select a card. I will wait for the score to load before continuing. You can also skip this step for an overview.'],
 ['Start with the score you have','Scores come in different forms: PDFs, photographs of printed pages, or sometimes just a recording. These import options let you start with what you already have. Photographs can be put in page order before processing.\n\nUploading a file is not the same as obtaining a usable score. I have encountered merged left- and right-hand parts and misread time signatures. The original is retained so that the electronic score can be checked. This matters especially in teaching research: otherwise we may mistake software errors for evidence about a student.'],
 ['Listen to a short passage','Choose a familiar passage, play it, then slow it down and follow the score. One recurring problem took me a long time to resolve: the notes appeared correct, but playback paused at the end of measures. Investigating meter and duration parsing showed why changing the piano sound alone could not help.\n\nThat experience made me more careful about expressive playback. Music needs room to breathe, but the beat must remain reliable. I want to establish accurate rhythm before shaping dynamics, phrase endings and the balance between the hands. Listen to whether the accompaniment supports the melody.'],
 ['What does a student do after seeing feedback?','This is the question I most want to investigate. A system can highlight wrong notes and timing errors, but has the student understood them, or simply followed the prompts once more? The next score alone cannot tell us.\n\nI would begin with a small comparison: the same passage and the same practice time, with one group receiving live prompts and another receiving feedback after each phrase. Then both play without prompts. Alongside errors, I would observe where they stop and restart. This is a proposed experiment, not a conclusion already reached. You can expand the practice controls here; microphone access must be enabled explicitly.'],
 ['Do these parts work together?','Here you can choose an ensemble and inspect the generated instrumental parts. My least satisfactory results are arrangements whose individual lines seem plausible but compete when combined. Checking instrumental ranges alone is clearly not enough.\n\nI want to pay closer attention to what happens after generation: how parts enter, how they leave space for the melody, and whether a student can actually play them. For teaching, I would rather develop a few dependable textures than offer many polished-looking arrangements that are difficult to use.'],
 ['Keep waiting in the background','This page looks technical, but it affects whether someone can use the system comfortably. Recognition and arranging take time. Blocking the page interrupts the musical idea a person was following. The queue allows users to inspect the original or listen to completed material while processing continues.\n\nThese are real tasks. You can see the piece and processing stage. When something fails, the system should explain where it stopped. Handling delays and errors is part of designing a tool for learning.'],
 ['A question I would like to discuss','I would like to return to remote MIDI duets, control network latency and compare compensation methods. Beyond measuring the time between two notes, I want to know whether performers are continually chasing one another or can still lead a musical phrase. I would value your thoughts on that distinction.']
 ];
 if(direction==='education'){
 translated[0][1]='I study music education. While building this website, I kept coming back to one question: after we point out a wrong note, how does a student practise the passage next time? I would like to show you the prototype first, then discuss which forms of feedback are worth investigating in the classroom.';
 translated[7][1]='I would start by comparing live feedback with feedback given after a phrase. With practice time held constant, can students still correct the passage when the prompts are removed? I think that matters more than an immediate improvement in their score. The experiment still needs careful design.';
 }else if(direction==='generation'){
 translated[0][1]='Generating a score that can be played is one thing; making its parts work together is much harder. This prototype keeps generation, listening and checking in one place. I would like to show you what works so far and where I am still getting stuck.';
 translated[7][1]='I would narrow the question to graded practice materials. If we preserve the structure of a piece and constrain its parts and difficulty, do the generated exercises become more useful for teaching? I would involve teachers through blind assessment and students through actual playing, rather than rely on the generating model to judge its own output.';
 }
 for(let i=0;i<steps.length;i++){if(!translated[i])continue;dictionary[steps[i][2]]=translated[i][0];dictionary[steps[i][3]]=translated[i][1];}
}
